{"kind":"request","text":"Give `ScopeHandle` a checked `log` read, so that a client outside the runtime can fold a scope's journal.\n\nFiled 2026-11-12 22:26 EST at Hugh's request, from the dap-foldkit plan (`~/play/dap-foldkit/PLAN.md`, draft 3 revision 3, change D1). Facts marked \"read\" are read at main `7ea170c9`. Facts marked \"M0\" come from `~/play/dap-foldkit/probe/RESULTS.md`, a probe that another session ran on 2026-11-12 against main `a76764a7`. I did not rerun it.\n\nWhy this matters. The plan runs dap's own judge and fold in the browser as a replica: `MemoryState`, `applyEntry` and `judgeAct` from `packages/derive`, fed by the scope's entries. The replica predicts the effect of each act before the scope answers, with no rule written twice. M0 shows that this works: a replica that folds 5,000 entries equals the scope's state, a prediction's effects equal the sealed entry's, and the fold takes 289 ms in headless Chrome (about 55 ms for each 1,000 entries). The replica needs the entries. Today a client has no supported way to read them.\n\nWhat exists (read).\n- `log(scope, reader, cursor)` is on the transport only: `packages/contract/src/transport.ts:91`, `packages/client/src/http.ts:150`. `ScopeHandle` (`packages/client/src/handle.ts`) has `history` and `entry`, and no `log`.\n- A member's session may read it: `MEMBER_READS` in `packages/scope/src/sessions.ts:121` names `log`.\n- The scope serves `LogPage { scope, definition, entries: { seq, hash, bytes }[] }` with `complete` and `next` (`packages/scope/src/reads.ts:301`). Below a floor it answers `archived` with the floor. It filters the rows by the session's limit (`#mine`, the same line group), so a page for a limited session can omit entries.\n- `ScopeHandle.entry` already checks one entry with `entryHash` (`handle.ts:179`).\n\nWhat to build.\n1. `ScopeHandle.log(cursor?, atLeast?)`, which returns `Read\u003cLogPage\u003e` and takes a receipt or a read minimum as the other reads do.\n2. Checks before the page is returned: the scope reference and the definition digest are the handle's, each entry's hash is the hash of its bytes, and the sequence numbers are contiguous from the cursor. A page with a gap is not returned as complete. The result says which case it is: whole, filtered by the session's limit, or below the floor.\n3. A statement in the client's documentation of when a member's page is whole, so that a caller knows when a fold of the pages equals the scope's state.\n\nNo change to the transport, the scope, the wire or the kernel. This is an existing read, exposed on the handle with the checks that every caller would otherwise write.\n\nSize: small. One method, its checks and its tests in `packages/client`.\n\nNeeded by: dap-foldkit M3 (a local dap room with two browsers). M2 runs on an in-memory scope and does not need it. dap-foldkit work continues to M2 without this head.\n\nNot verified: whether a plain member session of an application room has a limit that filters `log` rows. The request owes that answer, because a filtered page cannot feed a replica.","body":{"conditions":"ScopeHandle.log returns a checked LogPage: scope and definition match the handle, each entry hash equals the hash of its bytes, sequence numbers are contiguous from the cursor, and the result distinguishes a whole page, a page filtered by the session's limit, and a read below the floor. Tests in packages/client cover each case. The client documentation states when a member's page is whole. No change to the transport, the scope, the wire or the kernel. The gate passes.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}